<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yizhou Commandery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">109 BCE–225 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yizhou <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">Commandery</a> was a commandery created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> in 109 BCE on the lands of the Dian kingdom in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> set it up after Dian submitted, but allowed the Dian king to keep his title and gave him a gold seal. Its seat lay at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-dian">Dianchi</a> county near Lake Dian, and it remained a frontier unit of Chinese rule until the Shu Han state renamed it Jianning in 225.</p></div>
